/**
\class pdg::Part
\ingroup Animation
\brief Independently animated Sprite subsection, artwork holder, or mounting frame.

Create a Part with Sprite::createPart(name); names are unique within that Sprite.
Its local transform is relative to the Sprite, an explicit parent Part, or an
authored bone, binding or socket. setParentPart(), bindToBone(),
bindToAnimationBinding() and bindToAnimationSocket() select one frame without
changing local offsets. getBoneId() is always safe and returns boneId_None for
an unbound Part (including one whose parent follows a bone). Authored relationships
are scoped to the current rig and safely invalidate on replacement.

setDrawing() retains shared editable Drawing contents. setImage(image,localBounds)
retains an image in an explicit local rectangle. Artwork changes leave identity,
logical dimensions, programmed motion, parent/binding and physics untouched.
clearContent() removes artwork. getContentBounds() returns current artwork-local
bounds or transformed bounds in partSpace_Sprite/partSpace_World. Part content is
drawn after the owner's existing artwork, in Part creation order, with the owner's
opacity and composed affine transform. Imported bindings supply transforms; this
API does not duplicate or automatically replace imported artwork. Drawing still
copies Attributes for its individual elements.

\section part_mounting Attaching an independent Sprite

hostPart.attachSprite(child, placement, childMount) returns a new Part beneath
hostPart. This mounting Part retains the child and supplies an Animated attachment
offset; getAttachedSprite() and child.getAttachmentPart() query the relationship.
Animate the mounting Part to move the held object. The child root rejects competing
transform edits, while its own clips, internal Parts, opacity and layer order remain
independent. Attachment neither merges Part ownership nor steps the child twice.

partPlacement_Snap aligns the child's origin, or the optional childMount frame,
with the host. partPlacement_PreserveWorld keeps its current world placement.
childMount must belong to the child; its frame is sampled once when attaching.
The mount's local state is the only editable attachment offset. detachSprite()
releases the child with its last published world pose and leaves the mounting Part
available for reuse as an ordinary Part. Removal of the host socket or owning Sprite
also detaches. Layer removal currently detaches mounts because placement and
update scheduling require a shared layer.
Retain native Part/Sprite pointers with addRef/release
when keeping them after removal; script wrappers retain them automatically.

Both Sprites must share a layer (or both be outside layers). Cycles, an already
mounted child, singular placement, or a root transform requiring shear are rejected
before creating a mount. Independent Part artwork supports affine shear, but Sprite
roots do not. A later invalid follow placement holds the last valid child pose and
reports getAttachmentError(); valid placement clears it. Use uniform ancestor scales
or aligned axes when rotating mounts. Rigid attachments currently require a child
without a PhysicsBody; physical holding constraints are not supported by this attachment API. Release does not invent physical velocity.

\code{.cpp}
auto* socket = hero->createPart("weaponGrip");
socket->bindToAnimationSocket("grip");
auto* held = socket->attachSprite(sword, pdg::partPlacement_Snap);
held->rotateTo(0.3f, 0.25, pdg::easeInOutQuad);
held->detachSprite();
\endcode
\section part_ik Explicit Part-chain IK

solveIK(middle,tip,target,space,bendDirection,influence) solves two segments whose
joint offsets come from the local locations of a contiguous three-Part chain.
It requires neither artwork nor bones. The target defaults to owning-layer/world
coordinates; partSpace_Local means the root Part's parent frame. The root's local
scale must have equal absolute axes; ancestor transforms may be affine/reflected.
The bend direction is +1 or -1, and influence is in [0,1]. Unreachable targets clamp
to the available reach and return false. Invalid topology or dynamic bodies reject
the operation before changing either joint. Direct IK replaces rotation programming;
setIKTarget() and setIKDriveTarget() install recurring controllers, and
setIKLimits() supplies fixed or shared rotary limits. Controller takeover requires
explicit release with clearIKTarget().

\section part_physics Part bodies

An unbound Part can create its own PhysicsBody without a Sprite root body. It joins
the Chipmunk world on a physics-enabled layer and publishes the solved state once;
removal detaches the retained body back to basic integration. This currently covers
free bodies, shared constraints, contact reporting and kinematic target scheduling.
Use setupCollider() for explicit geometry, setupFrameCollider() for owned artwork
or an explicitly selected image binding, and setupAnimationCollider(boxName) for
one authored box. Bone binding alone does not create a physical joint or give the
body authority to write a bone. Generated animation physics requires Chipmunk.

*/
